// RED TEAM round 3, resource-dos lens (2026-08-06).
//
// A GUARD-FREE recursive-descent path, distinct from LEDGER item 14
// (parse_statement's stray-`;` recovery, same lens, different function)
// and distinct from item 22 (the parse_expr_bp_inner Pratt-loop lookahead
// overcharge, already fixed): a chain of `if let ... elif let ...`
// clauses.
//
// Read directly (compiler/crates/kryos-parser/src/parser.rs): every OTHER
// genuinely recursive parse entry point checks `self.nesting_exhausted()`
// (`MAX_NESTING_DEPTH = 2048`, `MAX_RECURSION_DEPTH = 256`) before
// recursing -- `parse_block` (~1169), `parse_expr_bp` (~1990),
// `parse_pattern` (~3302), `parse_type` (~3574). The if-let desugar chain
// is a FIFTH genuine recursive-descent cycle that checks nothing:
//
//   parse_if_rest -> (sees `let`) -> parse_if_let -> parse_if_let_else
//   -> (sees `elif`) -> parse_if_rest (RECURSE, zero guard anywhere
//      in this 3-function cycle)
//
// `parse_if_rest`'s own `while self.eat(Elif) { .. }` loop (a few lines
// above) DOES bound a chain of plain `elif`s with a loop, not recursion --
// but the moment an `elif` is followed by `let`, control re-enters
// `parse_if_rest` via `parse_if_let_else`'s explicit recursive call
// (parser.rs:1568), and NOTHING checks `nesting_exhausted()` anywhere in
// `parse_if_rest`/`parse_if_let`/`parse_if_let_else`. A flat chain of
// `elif let` clauses is therefore genuine, unbounded native Rust
// recursion, invisible to the guard that already protects four sibling
// entry points.
//
// Live repro, `compiler/target/release/kryos.exe`, no compiler changes,
// `KRYOS_STDLIB_DIR` set per repo convention. This 3-clause file is the
// harmless control (parses clean, reaches the checker, exit 1 with
// ordinary E0507/E0102 diagnostics -- no crash), proving the construct
// itself is legal and the parser accepts it up to this depth:
fn f() -> Option<i64> {
    return Some(1)
}

fn main() {
    if let Some(x) = f() {
        println("0")
    } elif let Some(x) = f() {
        println("1")
    } elif let Some(x) = f() {
        println("2")
    } else {
        println("done")
    }
}

// At scale the SAME construct crashes the compiler itself, on `kryos
// check` alone (before any codegen), well under any documented depth
// ceiling. Regenerate the bomb (impractical size to commit, mirrors item
// 14's convention) and run:
//
//   python3 -c "
//   n = 50000
//   lines = ['fn f() -> Option<i64> { return Some(1) }', 'fn main() {']
//   lines.append('    if let Some(x) = f() {')
//   lines.append('        println(\"0\")')
//   lines.append('    }')
//   for i in range(1, n):
//       lines.append('    elif let Some(x) = f() {')
//       lines.append('        println(\"%d\")' % i)
//       lines.append('    }')
//   lines.append('    else { println(\"done\") }')
//   lines.append('}')
//   open('bomb.kry', 'w').write(chr(10).join(lines))
//   "
//   $ kryos check bomb.kry
//   kryos: stack overflow (unbounded recursion?)
//   $ echo $?
//   253
//
// Proven both ways, live, this session, classified by EXIT CODE + the
// crash message (not by grepping source text -- this message is a fixed
// runtime string, not an echo of any source line, so it cannot be the
// documented false-positive-grep trap):
//   N=1000    (3,006-line file)   -- pending bisection at time of filing
//   N=50000   (150,006-line file) -- rc=253, "kryos: stack overflow
//                                     (unbounded recursion?)", ~real time
//   N=150000  (450,006-line file) -- rc=253, same crash
//   N=300000  (900,006-line file) -- rc=253, same crash
// `kryos check` crashes before typecheck/codegen is ever reached, so
// `run`/`build --release` are equally exposed (identical to item 14's
// blast radius) -- the crash happens during parsing itself.
//
// Fix shape (not attempted -- attacker-only mandate this round): the
// exact same guard already used 4 times in this file --
// `self.nesting_exhausted()` checked (and `rec_depth` bumped/popped)
// around the recursive `self.parse_if_rest(kw)` call in
// `parse_if_let_else` (parser.rs:1568 and 1584) -- degrades this to the
// existing clean E0010 diagnostic instead of an uncatchable native stack
// overflow. One-function-family fix mirroring the pattern already present
// elsewhere in the same file, same shape as item 14's fix.
